WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.204
When you're learning a new idea, for
example a new vocabulary word or

2
00:00:10.204 --> 00:00:13.594
a new concept, or
a new problem-solving approach,

3
00:00:13.594 --> 00:00:19.144
you sometimes tend to practice it over and
over again during the same study session.

4
00:00:19.144 --> 00:00:23.582
A little of this is useful and
necessary but continuing to study or

5
00:00:23.582 --> 00:00:29.598
practice after you've mastered what you
can in the session is called overlearning.

6
00:00:29.598 --> 00:00:31.606
Overlearning can have its place.

7
00:00:31.606 --> 00:00:36.196
It can help produce an automaticity
that can be important when you're

8
00:00:36.196 --> 00:00:40.622
executing a serve in tennis or
playing a perfect piano concerto.

9
00:00:40.622 --> 00:00:43.481
If you choke on tests or
in public speaking,

10
00:00:43.481 --> 00:00:46.510
overlearning can be especially valuable.

11
00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:50.359
Did you know that even expert
public speakers practice on

12
00:00:50.359 --> 00:00:54.920
the order of 70 hours 40 for
a typical 20 minute TED talk?

13
00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:59.680
Automaticity can indeed be helpful
in times of nervousness, but

14
00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:04.336
be wary of repetitive overlearning
during a single session.

15
00:01:04.336 --> 00:01:09.240
Research has shown it can be
a waste of valuable learning time.

16
00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:13.200
The reality is, once you've got
the basic idea down during a session,

17
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.954
continuing to hammer away at it during the
same session doesn't strengthen the kinds

18
00:01:17.954 --> 00:01:21.704
of long term memory connections
you want to have strengthened.

19
00:01:21.704 --> 00:01:26.814
Worse yet, focusing on one technique
is a little like learning carpentry.

20
00:01:26.814 --> 00:01:29.917
By only practicing with
a hammer after a while,

21
00:01:29.917 --> 00:01:33.526
you think you can fix anything
by just bashing at it.

22
00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:38.361
Using a subsequent study session to repeat
what you're trying to learn is just fine

23
00:01:38.361 --> 00:01:39.742
and often valuable.

24
00:01:39.742 --> 00:01:44.062
It can strengthen and
deepen your chunked neural patterns.

25
00:01:44.062 --> 00:01:45.358
But be wary.

26
00:01:45.358 --> 00:01:51.106
Repeating something you already know
perfectly well is, face it, easy.

27
00:01:51.106 --> 00:01:55.499
It can also bring the illusion of
competence that you've mastered the full

28
00:01:55.499 --> 00:01:59.874
range of material when you've actually
only mastered the easy stuff.

29
00:01:59.874 --> 00:02:04.232
Instead, you want to balance your
studies by deliberately focusing on

30
00:02:04.232 --> 00:02:06.290
what you find more difficult.

31
00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:12.210
This focusing on the more difficult
material is called deliberate practice.

32
00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:15.891
It's often what makes the difference
between a good student and

33
00:02:15.891 --> 00:02:17.500
a great student.

34
00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:22.324
All this is also related to
a concept known as Einstellung.

35
00:02:22.324 --> 00:02:28.277
In this phenomenon, your initial simple
thought, an idea you already have in mind,

36
00:02:28.277 --> 00:02:31.749
or a neural pattern you've
already developed and

37
00:02:31.749 --> 00:02:36.812
strengthened may prevent a better idea or
solution from being found.

38
00:02:36.812 --> 00:02:41.276
We saw this in the focused pinball
picture where your initial

39
00:02:41.276 --> 00:02:45.389
pinball thought went to
the upper part of the brain, but

40
00:02:45.389 --> 00:02:49.800
the solution thought pattern
was in the lower part.

41
00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:52.608
The crowded bumpers of
the focused mode and

42
00:02:52.608 --> 00:02:57.444
the previous patterns you've built can
create a sort of rut that prevents

43
00:02:57.444 --> 00:03:02.200
you from springing to a new place
where the solution might be found.

44
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:07.296
Incidentally, the German word
Einstellung means mindset.

45
00:03:07.296 --> 00:03:11.901
Basically, you can remember Einstellung
as installing a roadblock because of

46
00:03:11.901 --> 00:03:15.260
the way you were initially
looking at something.

47
00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:19.177
This kind of wrong approach is
especially easy to do in sports and

48
00:03:19.177 --> 00:03:23.966
science, not to mention other disciplines,
because sometimes your initial

49
00:03:23.966 --> 00:03:28.916
intuition about what's happening or
what you need to be doing is misleading.

50
00:03:28.916 --> 00:03:31.937
You have to unlearn your
erroneous older ideas or

51
00:03:31.937 --> 00:03:36.150
approaches even even while
you're learning new ones.

52
00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:40.736
One significant mistake students sometimes
make in learning is jumping into the water

53
00:03:40.736 --> 00:03:42.350
before they learn to swim.

54
00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:47.758
In other words, they blindly start working
on homework without reading the textbook,

55
00:03:47.758 --> 00:03:50.902
attending lectures,
viewing online lessons, or

56
00:03:50.902 --> 00:03:53.822
even speaking with someone knowledgeable.

57
00:03:53.822 --> 00:03:58.713
This is a recipe for sinking it's
like randomly allowing a thought to

58
00:03:58.713 --> 00:04:03.436
kind of pop off in the focus mode
pinball machine without paying any

59
00:04:03.436 --> 00:04:07.580
real attention to where
the solution truly lies.

60
00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:13.500
Understanding how to obtain real solutions
is important in learning and in life.

61
00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:18.380
Mastering a new subject means learning
not only the basic chunks, but

62
00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:22.388
also learning how to select and
use different chunks.

63
00:04:22.388 --> 00:04:27.330
The best way to learn that is by
practicing jumping back and forth between

64
00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:32.704
problems or situations that require
different techniques or strategies.

65
00:04:32.704 --> 00:04:35.424
This is called interleaving.

66
00:04:35.424 --> 00:04:40.150
Once you have the basic idea of a
technique down during your study session,

67
00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:43.827
sort of like learning to ride
a bike with training wheels,

68
00:04:43.827 --> 00:04:48.256
start interleaving your practice
with problems of different types or

69
00:04:48.256 --> 00:04:52.520
different types of approaches,
concepts, or procedures.

70
00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:55.336
Sometimes this can be
a little tough to do.

71
00:04:55.336 --> 00:04:57.818
A given section in a book, for example,

72
00:04:57.818 --> 00:05:02.490
is often devoted to a specific technique,
so when you flip to that section,

73
00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:06.680
you already know which technique
you're going to be using.

74
00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:09.920
Still, do what you can
to mix up your learning.

75
00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:14.284
In science and math in particular, it can
help to look ahead at the more varied

76
00:05:14.284 --> 00:05:17.936
problem sets that are sometimes
found at the end of chapters.

77
00:05:17.936 --> 00:05:22.871
Or you can deliberately try to make
yourself occasionally pick out why some

78
00:05:22.871 --> 00:05:26.746
problems call for
one technique as opposed to another.

79
00:05:26.746 --> 00:05:31.486
You want your brain to become used to
the idea that just knowing how to use

80
00:05:31.486 --> 00:05:37.098
a particular concept, approach, or
problem solving technique isn't enough.

81
00:05:37.098 --> 00:05:40.506
You also need to know when to use it.

82
00:05:40.506 --> 00:05:44.916
Interleaving your studies, making it
a point to review for a test, for

83
00:05:44.916 --> 00:05:49.694
example, by skipping around through
problems in the different chapters and

84
00:05:49.694 --> 00:05:54.619
materials, can sometimes seem to make
your learning a little more difficult,

85
00:05:54.619 --> 00:05:57.848
but in reality,
it helps you learn more deeply.

86
00:05:57.848 --> 00:06:01.296
Interleaving is extraordinarily important.

87
00:06:01.296 --> 00:06:02.801
Although practice and

88
00:06:02.801 --> 00:06:07.868
repetition is important in helping
build solid neural patterns to draw on,

89
00:06:07.868 --> 00:06:12.856
it's interleaving that starts
building flexibility and creativity.

90
00:06:12.856 --> 00:06:16.433
It's where you leave the world
of practice and repetition and

91
00:06:16.433 --> 00:06:19.070
begin thinking more independently.

92
00:06:19.070 --> 00:06:23.093
When you interleave within one subject or
one discipline,

93
00:06:23.093 --> 00:06:27.798
you begin to develop your creative
power within that discipline.

94
00:06:27.798 --> 00:06:32.930
When you interleave between several
subjects or disciplines, you can easily,

95
00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:37.158
more easily, make interesting new
connections between chunks in

96
00:06:37.158 --> 00:06:42.006
the different fields, which can
enhance your creativity even further.

97
00:06:42.006 --> 00:06:46.692
Of course, it takes time to develop solid
chunks of knowledge in different fields,

98
00:06:46.692 --> 00:06:48.856
so sometimes there's a trade off.

99
00:06:48.856 --> 00:06:52.522
Developing expertise in
several fields means you

100
00:06:52.522 --> 00:06:56.504
can bring very new ideas
from one field to the other.

101
00:06:56.504 --> 00:07:01.125
But it can also mean that your expertise
in one field or the other isn't quite as

102
00:07:01.125 --> 00:07:05.400
deep as that of the person who
specializes in only one discipline.

103
00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:10.240
On the other hand, if you develop
expertise in only one discipline,

104
00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:15.414
you you may know it very deeply, but
you may become more deeply entrenched

105
00:07:15.414 --> 00:07:21.170
in your familiar ways of thinking and
not be able to handle new ideas.

106
00:07:21.170 --> 00:07:25.200
Philosopher of science Thomas Kuhn
discovered that most paradigm shifts

107
00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:28.125
in science are brought about
by either young people or

108
00:07:28.125 --> 00:07:31.786
people who were originally trained
in a different discipline.

109
00:07:31.786 --> 00:07:32.765
They're not so

110
00:07:32.765 --> 00:07:38.196
easily trapped by Einstellung blocked
thoughts due to their preceding training.

111
00:07:38.196 --> 00:07:43.155
And of course, there's the old saying
that science progresses one funeral at

112
00:07:43.155 --> 00:07:48.220
a time as people entrenched in the old
ways of looking at things die off.

113
00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:52.384
Finally, don't make the mistake of
thinking that learning only occurs in

114
00:07:52.384 --> 00:07:55.828
the kinds of subjects you
acquire from teachers or books.

115
00:07:55.828 --> 00:08:00.129
When you teach a child how to
deal effectively with a bully,

116
00:08:00.129 --> 00:08:02.022
or you fix a leaky faucet,

117
00:08:02.022 --> 00:08:07.013
or you quickly pack a small suitcase for
a business trip to Hong Kong,

118
00:08:07.013 --> 00:08:12.478
all of these illustrate the outcomes
of important aspects of learning.

119
00:08:12.478 --> 00:08:17.260
Physicist, Richard Feynman was inspired
in his Nobel Prize winning work by

120
00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:21.582
watching someone throw a dinner
plate into the air in a cafeteria.

121
00:08:21.582 --> 00:08:26.122
And Mike Rowe of the television show's
Dirty Jobs and Somebody's Got to Do it,

122
00:08:26.122 --> 00:08:30.460
shows how important and exciting how
exciting learning can be in a variety of

123
00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:33.150
different nonacademic disciplines.

124
00:08:33.150 --> 00:08:34.838
I'm Barbara Oakley.

125
00:08:34.838 --> 00:08:36.592
Thanks for learning about learning.